### Beat 3 - Elaboration

#### Pre-Diplomatic Immunity

Alenawe:
*\[Deciding how much to say. Not reluctant — calculating.\]*
"Something keeps bothering me."
*\[Beat.\]*
"Every problem we've encountered — every one — has been solved by knowing the right person. Not by following procedure. Not by authority. Just... knowing someone."
"You walk into a room, find the person who matters, and make them need you. No credentials. No authority. Just... presence."
*\[Quieter. Almost to herself.\]*
"I was never taught that was real."

#### Post-Diplomatic Immunity

Alenawe:
*\[More direct than usual. Less filtering.\]*
"I used to think the way things work here — favors, connections, personal loyalty over procedure — was a local failing."
*\[Beat.\]*
"Something broke recently that suggests... it isn't local. I watched someone I respect bypass every protocol because it was personal."
"And it worked."
*\[Beat.\]*
"That shouldn't have worked."

*\[Note: "What if rules aren't the structure? What if they're just... the story the structure tells about itself?" — this line may belong in Beat 5 (the Slip) for the Post-DI variant. It is the most dangerous thing she says here and reads as the moment she crosses from analysis to observation. If it stays in the Elaboration, the Slip has nowhere further to go.\]*

